window.TYDE_BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Using Tyde", "url": "index.html", "text": "Learn the interface and the terms used in this guide. Start here with Tyde installed and open. This guide assumes Tyde is installed and open. It covers desktop Tyde and access from your phone . Choose a chapter for the task you are doing, or start with a conversation without a project . Find your way around the window Eight open demo agents across three projects. The selected conversation is working on a search improvement. Open the image for a closer look. Area What you do there Project rail, at the left Select Home or a project. The selected project determines which files you browse and the context for new project agents. Files and Git sidebar Expand folders, open files, inspect working changes, and browse commits for the selected project. Conversation and file panes Read an agent conversation or open source files. Conversation tabs let you return to agents you already have open. Agents and History sidebar Find active agents or saved sessions. Use the Agents filters to include other projects or hide inactive agents. Conversation composer Send requests and follow-ups. Responses and tool activity appear above it. Settings Configure hosts, backends, custom agents, skills, and integrations. Check the selected host before changing its configuration. In the screenshot, Garden is selected in the project rail, its folders are expanded under Files, and the center pane shows an agent reading files for a search change. The Agents sidebar also includes agents from Atlas API and Field Notes because Show other projects is enabled. Terms used in this guide Term Meaning Host The machine running Tyde’s server and the agent processes. The local host is this computer; a remote host is another machine you connect to. Backend The CLI harness running a conversation, such as Claude or Codex. It supplies the available models and backend-specific session options. Project A named workspace on one host with one or more root folders. Git roots have their own working changes and commit history. Agent A conversation backed by a harness. It may be working, waiting for input, or idle. An agent can start with or without a project. Session The saved conversation listed in History that you can resume later. Custom agent A saved configuration of instructions, skills, and tools used when launching an agent. Workbench A project derived from another project’s Git roots, with separate working trees and branches. Choose a task Start a conversation and learn the response, tool, and follow-up controls. Open code in a project , then review its changes . Find and organize agents , or resume an older session . Connect another machine and work with its projects, or pair your phone to access the same agents. Configure custom agents and skills , then use subagents . Use Settings reference or Troubleshooting for a specific control or problem. Controls and available options can differ by host and backend. Instructions that say to select a host refer to the machine whose files, agents, or settings you intend to use. "}, {"title": "Start and manage a conversation", "url": "first-agent.html", "text": "Choose a backend, start an agent, read its responses and tool activity, and send follow-up instructions. Check the backend on your host A backend is the CLI harness that runs your agent. Each conversation uses a selected backend; its available models and options appear in session settings. Open Settings → Backends . Check the host selector before changing settings. Choose the backend you want to use. Follow its installation or repair controls and any diagnostics shown by Tyde. Complete that backend’s authentication on the same machine where it will run. A login on your laptop does not automatically log in a remote server. Confirm the backend is available, and enable it for use. Choose a default if you want new conversations to start with it. Use your existing setup. If you already use a supported CLI harness, start with that backend. Tyde still needs access to its executable and authentication on the selected host. Choose New Chat on Home to start a conversation without a project. Manage Hosts opens connection settings. Start without a project Choose New Chat from the home screen or command palette. Use the launch selector to choose an available agent or backend. Look at the session settings before sending: the chosen backend determines which options appear. Send a small request that requires no file access, such as the example below. Read the response, then send a follow-up in the same conversation. Help me plan a small personal website. Ask me three questions about the audience and the pages I need. Don’t create any files yet. The response appears in the same tab. This conversation has no project roots. To browse a repository and review changes from a code task, open a project and start an agent there. Read responses and tool activity The transcript contains your messages, the agent’s responses, and tool calls made during the turn. A tool card shows an operation such as reading a file or running a command. Expand it to inspect the available arguments and result. Check whether it is still running or has finished before interpreting its output. The agent’s status describes the current turn. If it is waiting for an answer or permission, use the controls shown in the conversation. When the response is complete, send another message in the composer to continue with the same agent. Send a follow-up or interrupt a turn Give the agent a concrete outcome and any constraints it needs. When it asks a question or requests permission, respond through the conversation’s controls. Use the stop control when you need to interrupt a turn, then explain what should happen next. Messages sent during a turn can enter the queue. Check the queued-message controls when you need to change direction immediately rather than wait for the current work. The visible agent status and conversation help you distinguish active work from a finished response. For a code task, continue to Projects and files . For installation or authentication problems, see Backend unavailable . "}, {"title": "Projects and files", "url": "projects.html", "text": "Open a project, add repository roots, browse files, and start agents in the selected project. Open a project on the right host Use the project-opening control on Home or in the project rail. In the folder browser, choose the host that contains your code. The browser lists that host’s filesystem. Navigate to the folder you want and choose Open Project Here . Select the project in the rail. Open a new agent from that context so it works with the project’s roots. For example, open ~/projects/garden on your home server. You can browse that project from your laptop without maintaining a second checkout on the laptop. Add another root to a project A project can contain more than one root. This is useful when one task spans an application repository and a related service repository. Choose + root in the file explorer header, then select the additional folder in the browser and choose Add Root . Each Git root retains its own repository state. Check which root a change belongs to when staging, committing, or reviewing work. A multi-root project does not turn separate repositories into one repository. Roots and workbenches. Tyde prevents changing the folders of a workbench, or of a parent project that currently has workbenches. Finish and remove those workbenches before reorganizing the parent’s roots. Browse files and Git history Select the project, then open Files in the sidebar. Expand a root and its folders, then select a file to open it in a file tab. Use the file explorer’s hidden-files control if the file is not shown. Right-click a folder to search within it. Use Search to locate relevant code, or return to the conversation tab to ask the agent about a file. For supported languages, code intelligence adds symbol information, navigation, and references. See Code intelligence for setup and language support. The Git panel gives you a second way into the project: select a changed file to understand what is different. Use Recent commits to inspect work already committed. Start a project agent Begin with a request you can evaluate. Name the area of the project, describe the expected outcome, and tell the agent whether you want investigation or implementation. Read this project and explain how the home page is assembled. Identify the files involved and suggest one small accessibility improvement. Wait for my decision before editing. Follow the file references in the response. When you ask for a change, inspect the result in the Git panel and continue to Git changes and review comments . "}, {"title": "Git changes and review comments", "url": "review.html", "text": "Inspect working changes and commits, add file or inline comments, and submit feedback to an agent. Open a file, working change, or commit Tyde connects review feedback to source code. You can inspect a file as it exists now, uncommitted changes, or changes from Git history. What you need Where to begin Understand a file Open it from the file explorer. Use a file comment for feedback about the whole file, or an inline comment for a specific location. Review current edits Open the Git panel and select a file under Changes or Staged. Check the root and the diff before commenting. Review committed work Choose Recent commits in the Git root header, select the commit or range, and open a changed file. A real Git change and a saved draft comment in the Garden example. Open the image to inspect the controls. Read the change in context Use unified or side-by-side diff views to compare old and new code. Expand context when you need to understand the surrounding function. A short diff can hide assumptions elsewhere in the file, so open the source when the change deserves a broader look. For a multi-root project, confirm the repository as well as the filename. Review the actual changes rather than relying only on the agent’s explanation of them. Add and submit review comments Find the relevant line or use Comment on file for feedback that applies to the file as a whole. Write the expected behavior and explain why the current change misses it. Open Review comments to read the collected feedback across roots. Choose the recipient under Send feedback to , then use Submit review when the review is ready. Read the agent’s response and review the revised code. This returns an empty page when there are no results. Show an empty-state message with a way to clear the filters, so the user can recover without reloading. A draft comment is not a submitted review. Collect your feedback, check its target, and submit it deliberately. If AI suggestions are pending, accept or reject them before submitting. Stage and commit changes The Git panel separates staged changes, unstaged changes, and untracked files. Stage the files you intend to include, inspect the staged result, and write a commit message describing the change. In a multi-root project, repeat this for each repository involved. Use discard controls carefully: discarding a file’s working changes is different from rejecting a review suggestion. For parallel changes, workbenches give each task a separate working tree. "}, {"title": "Organize active agents", "url": "concurrency.html", "text": "Find conversations, filter the agent list, and follow agents working in different projects. Eight open demo agents across three projects. The selected conversation is working on a search improvement. Open the image for a closer look. Open and name an agent Select the project whose agents you want to see. Open the Agents sidebar and select an agent to open its conversation. Use the agent’s Rename agent control to give it a recognizable task name. Switch between open conversation tabs to return to agents you are following. For independent tasks, start separate conversations from the appropriate project. Naming them “Search empty state” and “API validation” makes the list easier to search than leaving both as New Chat. Filter the agent list Control Use it to Search field Find a named agent in the list. Hide inactive Reduce the list to active work. Turn it off to find idle agents again. Hide sub-agents Hide delegated children while looking through the parent agents. Turn it off to inspect a child. Show other projects Include agents outside the selected project. Check each agent’s project before sending a request. Changing these filters changes the list you see; it does not stop the hidden agents. At Home, the list can include projects by default. If an agent appears to be missing, clear the search and check these filters. Open Agent Monitor from the command palette for a dedicated view of concurrent agent activity. Inspect an agent’s status Find the agent in the list and read its status. Open the conversation to see the current response, tool call, or request for input. Answer any pending question using the conversation controls. If you need to stop the current work, interrupt the turn and send the corrected instruction. When a code task is complete, open its project’s Git panel to review the changes. Selecting another project or conversation leaves other agents running. Closing the client connection also leaves remote agents on their host; see Disconnect and return . Keep simultaneous edits understandable Two agents in the same project roots can touch the same working files. Tell agents which files or responsibilities they own. For independent implementation tasks, use separate workbenches and review each change before integrating it. Read-only investigation and review often work well alongside implementation, but reviewers need to know whether the files are still changing. Give them a stable commit or wait for the implementation to reach a reviewable point. "}, {"title": "History and saved sessions", "url": "sessions.html", "text": "Find and reopen an earlier conversation on the host where it ran. An agent and its session An agent is the live conversation you interact with. Its session is the saved conversation that lets you find and reopen earlier work. A saved session can exist when no agent is actively working on it. Disconnecting from a remote host is a third case: the agent may still be running on that host. Reconnect to inspect it rather than immediately launching a replacement. Resume from History Connect to the host where the original conversation ran. Open History and locate the conversation using its preview, project, backend, and last-active information. Use the resume action to reopen it. Read the restored conversation before sending a new request. Send a follow-up that states what you want to do next. Continue from the approach we chose yesterday. First check the current files and tell me whether anything has changed that affects the plan. Reopening a session should not be confused with asking it to do more work. The next message supplies that instruction. Files may have changed since the conversation was saved, so give the agent room to inspect their current state. Resume context and current files Use meaningful names and close a task with a concise decision: what was completed, what remains, and where the changes live. This makes it easier for you to choose the right conversation later. Context limits, compaction, and resume behavior depend on the harness. Tyde presents the session through a shared interface, but a long conversation still operates within its backend’s capabilities. If a conversation seems missing Check the selected host and any History filters first. Work performed on a home server belongs to that host; switching to the laptop’s local host does not move its sessions. See History and reconnect recovery before starting the task again. "}, {"title": "Workbenches", "url": "workbenches.html", "text": "Create a separate branch and working tree for a project task, then integrate and remove the finished workbench. What a workbench contains A workbench is a project connected to a parent project’s Git roots, with its own branch and working trees. It gives an independent change room to develop without mixing its edits into the parent checkout. Use one for a feature, a bug fix, or another bounded task. For a multi-root project, the workbench follows the parent’s roots so related repository work stays together. Create and open a workbench Select a project with Git roots and open its context menu. Choose New Workbench . Provide a new branch name that describes the change, such as feature/search-empty-state . Select Create Workbench , wait for creation to complete, then open it. Start the implementation agent from the workbench context. The new workbench starts from committed repository state. Uncommitted and untracked changes in the parent are not copied into it. Commit any prerequisites you intend the workbench to contain before creating it. Work and review inside it Keep implementation, project checks, and review in the workbench. Check its branch and Git changes before committing. If several agents share a workbench, they still share that workbench’s files; agree on responsibilities. For two independent features, create two workbenches. This makes it easier to review each diff and decide when each change is ready. Integrate, then remove Review the result and run the checks required by your project. Commit the completed change on the workbench branch. Integrate the commits using your repository’s normal Git workflow. Creating a workbench does not automatically merge its changes. Confirm the intended commits are present in the destination branch. Open the workbench’s context menu and choose Remove Workbench . Removal is a cleanup action. Finish the work and preserve its commits first. Removing a workbench also removes its associated active workspace resources. Do not force removal to get past uncommitted work you still need. "}, {"title": "Connect remote hosts", "url": "remote.html", "text": "Add an SSH host, configure its backends, open its projects, and reconnect to its running agents. Where files and agents run A remote host runs Tyde’s server, the CLI backends, and the agents that use them. Your desktop client connects to that host to display state and send your actions. You can connect to more than one host from the same laptop. When you disconnect the client, agents can continue on the remote host. When you reconnect, Tyde receives the host’s current workspace state. This depends on the host and its processes staying running; disconnecting your laptop is different from shutting down the server. Client and remote hosts Your laptop Connected to both hosts Home server Personal projects Website · Claude Working Tools · Codex Working Work server Work projects API · OpenCode Working Dashboard · Hermes Working Disconnect laptop Pause motion Agents run on their hosts. Your laptop connects you to their work. Interactive concept diagram. These example agents illustrate where work runs. Connect your first server Make sure you can access the server through SSH. Use the account that should own the projects and agent processes. Open Settings → Hosts → Add Remote Host . Give the host a recognizable label, such as Home server , and enter its SSH destination , such as developer@home-server . Leave Remote command blank for managed installation and launch of the matching Tyde release. Use a custom command only when you manage the bridge yourself. Choose whether to auto-connect on launch, then select Add Host . Connect and wait for the connected state. Select this host in settings. Open Backends and install or authenticate the harnesses you want to run there. Keep the host selector in view. Backend installation, skills, custom agents, and host settings apply to the selected machine. Your laptop’s CLI login and filesystem paths are not the remote server’s configuration. Hosts settings: choose the machine whose configuration you want to manage, or add an SSH connection. Open the remote code Open the project browser and select your new host. Navigate to a repository on that server and choose Open Project Here . Start an agent within the project and give it a bounded task. Code browsing, Git changes, and review now refer to files on the server. You do not need to copy those files onto your laptop to inspect them in Tyde. Add a home server and a work server Repeat the host setup for your second server. Use clear labels and open the appropriate projects under each host. For example, keep a personal website on Home server and an application plus its API on Work server. Move between the projects to inspect their agents. Before changing shared configuration, check the settings host selector. Before starting a new conversation, check its host and project context. Disconnect and return Leave a known task running on a remote host. Use that host’s Disconnect control. This closes the client connection. Reconnect using Connect . Open the original agent and inspect the work that happened while you were away. If you reconnect after a host restart, inspect History and the actual process state. Do not assume that an interrupted server process continued merely because its conversation was saved. For mobile access, Tyde also has host pairing controls in settings. See mobile access for the distinction between connecting a desktop over SSH and pairing a mobile client. "}, {"title": "Use Tyde on your phone", "url": "mobile.html", "text": "Pair your phone with a host, continue the same agent conversations, start chats, resume sessions, and manage notifications. What the mobile app connects to Tyde mobile is a web app that connects to the Tyde host running on your computer or server. Its Agents list shows agents on that host, including conversations you opened on desktop. Open an existing agent to continue that conversation; you do not need to start another agent on the phone. The host runs the CLI harnesses and holds the project files and sessions. Your phone displays the conversation and sends your actions to the host. Keep the host running, awake, and reachable. Closing the phone app does not shut down agents on the host. Pair through the cloud In desktop Tyde, open Settings → Mobile . Check the host selector: choose the computer or server whose agents you want on your phone. Turn on Enable mobile connections , then choose Pair via cloud . Tyde displays a pairing QR and its expiry. On your phone, open Tyde mobile and choose the QR scanning control. Allow camera access when prompted and scan the QR on your desktop. If scanning is unavailable, expand Show pairing URI on desktop and paste it into the phone’s pairing-code field. Choose Connect . If Tyde displays Show pairing link instead, open that link on your phone. Complete the phone’s Tyggs sign-in and Pass flow. Managed cloud pairing requires a Tyggs Pass. Complete the pairing prompts, then wait for the host to show as connected on the phone. Open Agents to find your conversations. A pairing offer expires after a short time. If it expires, create a new one on desktop. Pairing links and codes grant access to the selected host; keep them private. Add the web app to your Home Screen You can open Tyde mobile in a browser or add it to your phone’s Home Screen using the browser’s installation or sharing controls. Open the installed app and complete pairing there if it asks you to connect again. On iPhone, use the Home Screen app to enable notifications. Continue a desktop conversation Select the paired host that owns the conversation. Open Agents and tap the agent’s name. Wait for its conversation to load. Read the transcript. Expand tool cards when you need to inspect what the agent ran and its result. The header shows the agent’s current status. Type in the composer and choose Send for a follow-up. When a turn is already running, check the send-action controls: a message can be queued rather than sent as a new idle turn. Use Stop to interrupt a running turn, or answer a question through the controls shown in the conversation. Tap Agents in the header to return to the list. Switching conversations does not stop other agents. The conversation view with demo data: agent and harness at the top, messages in the transcript, and a follow-up draft in the composer. Tap the image to enlarge it. The composer also supports photo attachments when the selected harness supports image input. Use the attachment control, inspect the attached images, and send them with your message. Voice controls depend on voice being configured on the host; ordinary text chat does not require voice setup. Find chats and saved sessions Tab What you do there Home Check the selected host and connection overview. Agents Open an agent, choose New chat, or switch to the Teams view. Sessions Find a saved conversation and choose Resume. Load older sessions when the conversation is not in the first page. Settings Choose the theme and tool-output display, manage notifications, and inspect the selected host’s configuration. To start work from mobile, choose New chat in Agents, select an available harness and agent configuration, and send the first request. The new agent runs on the selected host and appears on desktop too. Any workspace folders you select belong to that host’s filesystem. To resume earlier work, use Sessions → Resume , read the restored conversation, and then send your next instruction. Desktop calls its saved-session view History ; mobile calls it Sessions . Enable agent notifications Open Settings → Notifications on the phone. On iPhone, launch Tyde from its Home Screen icon first. Choose Enable notifications and allow the browser’s permission request. Check that Agent idle alerts shows On . If permission is already granted but the device is not subscribed, choose Turn on . Use Turn off in the same section when you no longer want alerts. Alerts notify you when an agent finishes a turn or asks you something. If the browser reports notifications as blocked, change that site’s notification permission in browser settings. If it reports them as unavailable, follow the displayed instructions for your browser. Reconnect and recover a message When you return to the phone app, check the host’s connection state before sending more work. A disconnected phone does not prove that an agent stopped: reconnect to the original host and inspect the existing conversation. If Tyde displays a message-recovery control after a connection failure, read its status before resending. A message whose delivery is uncertain may already have reached the host. Check the conversation first; use the offered recovery action deliberately rather than repeatedly tapping Send. If your agents or sessions seem missing, check the selected host. Pair each additional computer or server separately. Files and conversations remain with the host where they were created. Connect through your own network Direct hosting serves the mobile web app from your own host instead of using managed cloud pairing. The phone must be able to reach the host’s address, for example on your network or VPN. On desktop, open Settings → Mobile for the target host and enable mobile connections. Turn on Host the mobile app from this machine . Configure the listening address and an HTTPS proxy for the host, then set Address phones use to the HTTPS address the phone can reach. The host serves HTTP behind the proxy; the phone needs the HTTPS address for browser features. Wait for the direct-hosting status to report that it is serving. Released hosts include the mobile web bundle; use the bundle setting only if you are supplying a separately built bundle. Choose Pair via local server , open its pairing link or scan the QR on the phone, and complete the direct-pairing flow. The cloud flow’s Tyggs Pass sign-in is separate from direct pairing. Use the local-server pairing control for direct access, and make sure the phone can reach that address when away from your desk. Remove a phone’s access In desktop Settings → Mobile , find the phone under Paired devices , choose Remove , and confirm. That phone needs a new pairing before connecting again. To stop all mobile access to the selected host, turn off Enable mobile connections . "}, {"title": "Custom agents, skills, and tools", "url": "agents-skills.html", "text": "Create reusable agent configurations, add skill files, and choose the tools each custom agent receives. Create a custom agent A custom agent is a preset: instructions, skills, MCP servers, and tool policy. Give it a role that you will use repeatedly, such as an accessibility reviewer or a maintainer familiar with your project conventions. Open Settings → Custom Agents and confirm the selected host. Create a definition with a recognizable name and a short description. Write instructions describing its responsibility, expected output, and boundaries. Select the skills and MCP servers it should receive, and review its tool policy. Save the definition and choose it when launching a new conversation. You review user-facing changes for accessibility. Explain the impact on keyboard and screen-reader users. Cite the relevant file and location for every finding. Propose concrete corrections and do not edit files unless asked. Add a skill on the host Skills are discovered from the filesystem. On the host where your agents run, create a folder under ~/.tyde/skills/ and put its instructions in SKILL.md . Open Settings → Skills and choose Refresh to re-scan. ~/.tyde/skills/ └── project-review/ └── SKILL.md A small skill can capture a review procedure: --- name: project-review description: Review this project's user-facing changes --- Read the project instructions before reviewing. Explain each finding with its user impact and file location. Check loading, empty, error, and keyboard-interaction states. Return findings in priority order, with a proposed correction. Refresh the list, then assign the skill to the custom agents that need it. The default agent uses all host skills; a custom definition lets you choose its skill set. How backends receive shared configuration Tyde makes host skills and agent definitions available through its backend integrations. This lets you maintain common instructions while choosing different harnesses for different tasks. Shared configuration does not mean every harness has identical tools, model options, permissions, or skill behavior. Check the session’s backend and capabilities, and keep instructions clear about the desired outcome. Host scope and MCP servers Custom agent changes are saved on the selected host. Adding a skill to your laptop does not add it to a remote host. If you want the same specialist on two servers, maintain the definitions and skill files on both. MCP servers add tools or resources to the agent. Configure them under Settings → MCP Servers , then choose which ones a custom agent should receive. Configure executable paths and credentials for the host that runs them. "}, {"title": "Subagents and teams", "url": "delegation.html", "text": "Configure delegation, ask an agent to create a Tyde subagent, and inspect the delegated conversation. Tyde subagents and backend-native subagents Some CLI harnesses have their own subagent mechanisms. A Tyde subagent is created through Tyde’s agent-control tools and appears as a managed agent in the studio. It can use a different supported backend from its parent. For example, a Codex agent can delegate an investigation to a Claude agent. The agents still run through their respective harnesses; Tyde provides the coordination and visibility around them. Prepare for delegation On the selected host, configure the backends you want available for delegated work. Review your custom agent’s tool configuration so it can use Tyde’s agent-control capabilities. Check the host’s launch profiles and delegation preferences. These help determine which configured backend and cost profile a child uses. Start the parent in the appropriate project or workbench. Delegation does not create credentials or install missing harnesses. A requested child needs an available configuration on the host where it will run. Ask an agent to delegate Investigate this issue. Use a Tyde subagent on another available backend to independently review the relevant code. Give it a read-only task with a clear question. Compare the findings and propose a fix before editing. Follow the child agent in Tyde. Open its conversation when you need to inspect its assumptions or results. Ask the parent to reconcile disagreements rather than treating two agent responses as automatic agreement. For implementation, specify file ownership or use workbenches. Cross-backend delegation does not isolate working files by itself. Create a team A team groups a manager and members with assigned roles. Its configuration is separate from a custom agent definition or a one-off subagent. Open the Teams panel on the host you want to use and choose + New team . Choose Start blank , Generate balanced team when available, or a listed template. Enter the Team name and review the draft members and their configurations. Use + Add report to add a member. Select Create team to save the draft, or Discard to abandon it. Open the manager’s conversation and describe the task you want the team to perform. Inspect member conversations to follow their work. Check the selected host’s available backends when configuring members. Creating the team defines its members; send the manager a request to assign work. Review a delegated result Ask for concrete evidence, inspect the resulting changes, and verify the outcome using your project’s normal checks. Delegation increases the amount of work you can run concurrently; your acceptance criteria still determine when it is finished. "}, {"title": "Step-by-step examples", "url": "workflows.html", "text": "Follow complete examples that combine projects, remote hosts, review, workbenches, and custom agents. Work on projects on two remote hosts Outcome: keep personal and work projects running on their respective machines while using one laptop to direct and review them. Connect Home server and Work server . Configure the desired backends on each. Open your personal website on Home server. On Work server, open the application project and add its API repository as a second root if the task spans both. Start a website agent on Home server and an API investigation agent on Work server. Give each a separate deliverable. Move between the projects and check their conversations. Respond to questions and review completed work. Disconnect the laptop while the remote hosts remain running. Reconnect later and open the original agents. Inspect their results before assigning more work. What to notice: the laptop is your connection to the work. Project files, backend authentication, and agent execution belong to each host. Implement with one agent, review with another Outcome: get an independent reading of a change before accepting it. Create a workbench for the change and launch an implementation agent inside it. Describe the expected behavior and your acceptance criteria. Ask the agent to report when the change is ready for review. Once the files are stable, ask for a Tyde subagent using another available backend to review the implementation without editing. Compare the review findings with the actual diff. Add your own inline comments. Submit the review to the implementation agent, then inspect the revision. Run the project’s required checks, commit, and integrate the finished work. Review the completed change independently. Focus on empty input, failed requests, and keyboard navigation. Cite concrete locations. Do not edit files or start another implementation. Create and use a custom reviewer Outcome: reuse your review standards without retyping them for every conversation. Create a project-review skill on the host. Refresh Skills in settings. Create a custom reviewer definition and assign the skill. Give it a clear output format and appropriate tool access. Launch the reviewer in a project and give it a specific change to inspect. Refine the instructions based on the usefulness of its findings. Use the same definition when launching later reviews on that host. Use the chapter links in each example for the individual controls and their settings. "}, {"title": "Settings reference", "url": "settings.html", "text": "Find host and session settings, backend setup, code intelligence, keyboard shortcuts, and mobile pairing. Check the scope first Host-scoped settings apply to the machine selected in the settings host selector. This includes Backends, Custom Agents, MCP Servers, and Skills. When managing more than one server, check the host label before saving. Session settings apply to the conversation’s backend. Choose a backend before the first message and inspect the options shown for it. A host default affects new conversations; it does not turn an existing conversation into a different harness. Backend setup and compatibility The current desktop interface includes Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Grok, Antigravity, Hermes, and Kiro integrations. Use Settings → Backends on the target host to see availability and diagnostics. An integration appearing in the interface does not mean its CLI is installed or authenticated on every machine. Tyde provides The harness still determines Conversation and workspace UI Available models and provider authentication Agent organization and session access Native context limits and supported session operations Shared agent and skill configuration Native tools and how instructions are applied Tyde delegation and launch preferences Provider capacity, cost, and capability limits Use launch profiles when you need repeatable backend choices for delegated work. Review the ordered delegation preferences and cost profiles rather than assuming every child will use the parent’s configuration. Code intelligence Tyde has language-server integrations for Rust through rust-analyzer and Python through Pyright . They provide source navigation, hover information, references, and diagnostics when the language server and project are available. Open the code-intelligence settings on the project’s host. If automatic discovery cannot find the server, set its executable path and follow the displayed diagnostics. Python support detects .py and .pyi files with project markers such as pyproject.toml or requirements.txt . Rust projects use Cargo.toml . Other file types can still be browsed. Syntax highlighting by itself does not imply language-server navigation support. Commands and keyboard access The command palette lists available actions and their platform-specific shortcuts. Use it to find New Chat, settings, the Agent Monitor, and workspace navigation without memorizing every control. Action macOS Other desktop platforms New Chat ⌘ N Ctrl N Toggle Settings ⌘ , Ctrl , Focus primary pane ⌘ 1 Ctrl 1 Focus secondary pane ⌘ 2 Ctrl 2 Mobile access Open Settings → Mobile on desktop to enable mobile connections, pair a phone, manage paired devices, or configure direct hosting. Mobile access connects the phone to the same agents running on the selected host. See Use Tyde on your phone for cloud and direct pairing, conversations, sessions, notifications, and recovery steps. Mobile pairing is separate from adding a desktop SSH host. Other settings Settings also include steering instructions, voice options, and agent activity features. Configure these for your workflow after the basic backend and project setup works. For any provider-backed feature, review its required credentials and usage implications on the selected host. "}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html", "text": "Resolve backend setup, connection, session, skill, navigation, and review-submission problems. Backend unavailable Check the selected host. A CLI installed locally may be missing remotely. Open Settings → Backends and read the setup status and diagnostic. Confirm the CLI can run and is authenticated under the account used by that host. Use the displayed install or repair controls, then refresh or reconnect as appropriate. Start a small conversation before returning to a larger task. Keep the exact error message when reporting a problem. “Installed but unavailable” is a different situation from “not installed,” and the diagnostic helps identify the next step. Host disconnected or reconnecting Open Settings → Hosts and inspect the connection state. Confirm that the machine is reachable and that SSH works for the configured destination. If you use a custom remote command, check that it is valid on the remote machine. A reconnecting state does not prove the agent has stopped. Reconnect and inspect the existing agent before starting a duplicate task. If the server itself restarted or stopped, check the restored session and current process state before continuing. History or agents seem missing Select the original host and check filters in the Agents or History view. If the agent is still running remotely, reconnect to it. If you want an earlier saved conversation, resume it from History. Check the project and backend details before reopening a similarly named conversation. When resuming old work, ask the agent to inspect current files before relying on its earlier assumptions. A skill is not available Confirm the skill file is on the host where the agent runs, under ~/.tyde/skills/<name>/SKILL.md . Open Skills settings on that host and choose Refresh. Confirm the skill appears in the list. Check that the custom agent definition includes the skill. Start a new conversation with the intended definition to evaluate the updated configuration. Code navigation is missing Check that the file is Rust or Python and belongs to a recognized project. Open code-intelligence settings on the host and inspect language-server availability. Verify the configured executable path if one is set. Opening a file and highlighting its syntax can work even when the language server is unavailable. Report the provider diagnostic, file type, and project marker when asking for help. A review will not submit Check whether the review is still a draft, whether it contains comments, and whether AI suggestions still need a decision. Accept or reject pending suggestions, choose the feedback target, and inspect any error shown when you submit. Confirm you are looking at the intended source: working changes and a historical commit are different review contexts. Report a useful issue Open an issue in the Tyde repository . Include the Tyde version, operating system, backend and CLI version, whether the host is local or remote, the steps you took, and what you expected to happen. Add a screenshot or exact error when it helps explain the problem. Remove credentials, private code, and personal paths from anything you publish. "}];
